<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daylam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dailam, Deylam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daylam was the mountainous region south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in the highlands of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> and Qazvin provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a>, long resisted Arab conquest. From the 9th century it was ruled by local dynasties such as the Justanids, and in the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> from Daylam took control of western Iran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daylam.jpg" alt="Daylam" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seyyed Shahabo-ddin Vajedi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nature_of_Deylaman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
